Image: evo-nexlab:sealed, linux/arm64, network none, digest
sha256:e8d52bdced1fa49a5a56cde7e1173c54bd81832cc111441f6a31148ec03ead93.
Compiler: gcc-14 (Debian 14.2.0-19) 14.2.0.

Command, for LEVEL in -O0 and -O2, KIND in 0, 1 and 2 and FOLD in 0 and 1:
  gcc-14 -std=gnu11 LEVEL -w -ffp-contract=off -DKIND=KIND -DFOLD=FOLD
         floating-half-fold.c -o t && ./t

LEVEL -O0 and -O2 print the same lines. A fold record is the run-time form
(FOLD=0), which is the reference: EvoCC gives these lines in both forms. A
gcc-fold record is GCC's translation-time form (FOLD=1). It differs from
GCC's own run time in two ways. An invalid operation, such as 0 * -inf,
folds to a NaN with the sign of the exact product or quotient, where the
machine gives the default NaN; this changes mul and div of every kind.
An __fp16 sum or difference with a negative NaN operand folds to a
positive NaN; this changes add and sub of KIND=1, and mul and div there
too. EvoCC folds as it executes, as it does for float, where GCC 14 folds
0.0f * -inf to 0xffc00000 and EvoCC to 0x7fc00000. The machine-read lines
follow the marker.

